        <div id="hose-sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Measure First: The Three Shop Vac Hose Sizes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every shop vac attachment sold in the US fits one of three hose diameters: 1-1/4 inch, 1-7/8 inch or 2-1/2 inch. These are nominal sizes, not exact measurements, and that is where most wrong orders start. The 1-1/4 inch size is found on 1 to 2.5 gallon vacs and on detailing hoses. The 1-7/8 inch size is common on 4 to 9 gallon vacs, such as the RIDGID 4000RV and DEWALT DXV04T in our <a href="/best-small-shop-vac/">small shop vac guide</a>. The 2-1/2 inch size is standard on most 9 to 16 gallon vacs, including the large models in our main <a href="/best-shop-vac/">best shop vac guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure before you buy. Take a tape measure or caliper to the outside of your wand or hose cuff, the end that tools slide onto. A 1-1/4 inch system measures roughly 1-1/4 inches across the outside of the wand. A 1-7/8 inch system measures close to 1-7/8 inches, and a 2-1/2 inch system close to 2-1/2 inches. If the number falls between sizes, it is usually because the hose cuff is tapered. Most tools are friction fit, meaning they push on and stay by being tight, so a small taper is normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second check is whether your vac uses a locking hose. RIDGID's newer vacs use a locking hose, and RIDGID's locking accessories are described as fitting both 1-7/8 and 2-1/2 inch hoses and wands. Locking hoses still accept friction-fit tools, but the lock only works with the matching brand's locking accessories. Shop-Vac, Craftsman, Vacmaster and WORKSHOP mostly use friction-fit tools, so their kits are more interchangeable between brands at the same diameter.
          </p>

