                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dust Deputy 2.5 DIY (AXD251004)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tall cyclone cone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-1/2 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cut lid, mount cone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Big vacs where height is not an issue</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dustopper PRO (DT10000 kit)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low bucket lid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-1/2 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Snap onto bucket</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Big vacs, under-bench setups</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Both makers claim high efficiency; Dustopper listings quote more than 99% and more than 99.9% of typical shop dust. We have not seen an independent test that compares them directly, so do not choose on those numbers. Choose on hose size and space. For a small vac with a 1-1/4 or 1-7/8 inch hose, the Dust Deputy DIY with its reducers is the natural fit. For a big 2-1/2 inch vac, either the Dust Deputy 2.5 or the Dustopper PRO works, and the Dustopper is the easier setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical notes. A plain 5 gallon bucket can collapse under a strong vac's suction, especially a thin-walled one, so use a sturdy bucket and do not block the hose with the vac running. And plastic separators build static, which is why both makers use anti-static resin and why Dustopper includes a grounding lug for 5.0 HP and larger vacs. If you get shocks off the hose, ground the system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Brand Compatibility: Will It Fit My Vac?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Friction-fit tools of the same nominal size are mostly interchangeable across brands. A WORKSHOP 2-1/2 inch claw nozzle will usually fit a Shop-Vac, Craftsman or Vacmaster 2-1/2 inch wand, and the same goes at 1-7/8 and 1-1/4 inch. The fit may be a little loose or tight, because there is no single industry tolerance, but most tools push on and stay.
          </p>

          <p>
            RIDGID is the brand most likely to cause confusion, for two reasons. First, its newer vacs use locking hoses and locking accessories, and the lock only works with RIDGID's locking tools. Friction-fit tools still work, just without the lock. Second, RIDGID's 1-1/4 inch car kit tools are sized for their own hose, so they do not fit RIDGID's full-size wands without an adapter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose cuffs are less interchangeable than tools. The end of the hose that plugs into the vac's inlet varies a lot by brand and model, and a replacement hose from another brand may not fit. If you are replacing or adding a hose, buy one from your vac's maker or one sold for your vac's model number. RIDGID's VT1720 Tug-A-Long, for example, is a 7 foot hose that runs from 1-1/4 inch at the handle to 1-7/8 inch at the vac, sold for its 4 and 6 gallon models.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, look at the accessory maker's compatibility list, and measure. A friction-fit tool that is slightly loose can be fixed with a wrap of electrical tape on the wand; one that is too tight to push on cannot. Ordering by measured diameter rather than vac brand saves most returns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hoses-wands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Hoses and Wands: Length, Cuffs and When to Upgrade
          </h2>

          <p>
            A longer hose is the most underrated shop vac upgrade. Every vac comes with a hose that is a compromise between reach and airflow, and for jobs like cleaning a car, a staircase or a ceiling, 6 to 8 feet is not enough. A longer hose loses some airflow, roughly in proportion to its length, but the gain in reach often makes the vac far more useful.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stay at your vac's hose diameter or larger when you add length. A long 1-1/4 inch hose on a big vac loses much more airflow than the same length at 1-7/8 or 2-1/2 inch. The exception is detailing, where a 10 foot 1-1/4 inch hose like the one in the RIDGID VT2534 is the point: it is light, flexible and its narrow bore speeds up the air at the nozzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wands deserve the same attention. Most vacs ship with two plastic wands that slip together. They flex, they separate, and they crack where they join. Metal wands with locking joints, like the stainless pair in the RIDGID VT2575, make floor work faster because you can push and pull hard without the wand coming apart. A bent wand, also in the VT2575, makes floor work easier on your back because you stand straighter.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hose with a bleeder valve is a small upgrade worth knowing about. RIDGID's VT2505 hose grip has one: a slide that opens a small hole to cut suction when a rug or plastic bag gets sucked onto the nozzle. For gutters, specialist kits like WORKSHOP's WS25051A combine a 90 degree elbow, two extension wands and a blower nozzle to reach a gutter from the ground. And for big piles of dry debris, Shop-Vac's 8017800 Bulk Dry Pick-Up Kit is a 2-1/2 inch hose, wand and utility nozzle set sized for leaves and chips.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What Not to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            The worst buys in this category are the generic 20 to 30 piece "universal" kits that promise to fit every vac. They usually include several cone adapters of soft plastic, a handful of small tools and a short hose, and the adapters are the problem: stack two or three to reach your vac's size and you have three leaky joints and a tool that falls off when you pull it across a floor. A smaller kit at your exact diameter, like the WORKSHOP WS25054A, works better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip a cyclone separator if your vac sees mostly water and loose debris. A separator earns its keep on dust and sawdust. For water, it adds nothing except a bucket to empty, and some separators are not designed for wet pickup at all. For a car vac, it is unnecessary bulk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy attachments to fix a vac that is losing suction. A clogged filter, a full drum, a cracked hose or a loose lid gasket will all make a vac feel weak, and no nozzle fixes that. Check those first. Once the vac is working, attachments make it better at the job you bought it for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be careful with bargain replacement parts sold as compatible with a brand. Many are fine, but cuff sizes vary and some tools are thinner plastic than the originals. For tools you will lean on, such as wands and floor nozzles, the vac maker's own parts or a reputable accessory maker like WORKSHOP or POWERTEC are worth the small premium. For a crevice tool you will use twice a year, the cheap one is fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering by Vac Brand Instead of Hose Diameter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brands use different sizes across their range, so a 'RIDGID' accessory can be 1-1/4, 1-7/8 or 2-1/2 inch. Measure the outside of your wand or hose cuff with a tape or caliper and order by that number. Most returns in this category are diameter mistakes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking Several Adapters to Make a Tool Fit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each joint leaks air, and soft cone adapters flex. Two or three in a row can cost more suction than a cracked hose, and the tool falls off on a floor. Buy a kit at your hose size, or use a single proper reducer like the one in the RIDGID VT2575.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Big Floor Nozzle on a Small Hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2-1/2 inch floor nozzle on a 1-1/4 inch hose starves the nozzle of air and barely picks up. Reduce down for small, fine jobs such as vents and seams, never up. For floors, use a tool that matches the vac's full hose diameter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping a Separator on a Sawdust Vac</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fine dust clogs a vac's filter in minutes and is the main reason shop vacs lose suction. A Dust Deputy or Dustopper on a 5 gallon bucket catches most of it first, keeps the filter clean and saves on filters and bags. On a vac used for sanding or woodworking, it pays for itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Thin Bucket Under a Separator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin-walled bucket can collapse under a strong vac's suction when the hose is blocked. Use a sturdy 5 gallon bucket, seal the lid properly and keep the hose clear. Ground plastic systems if you get static shocks, especially on 5.0 HP and larger vacs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

