        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Measure your hose first, then buy for the job. For a better all-round kit on a 1-7/8 or 2-1/2 inch vac, the RIDGID VT2575 is the one to buy, with locking stainless wands, a squeegee floor nozzle and adapters for both sizes; the WORKSHOP WS25054A is the budget answer at 2-1/2 inch. For cars, the RIDGID VT2534 with its own 10 foot 1-1/4 inch hose is the easiest kit to use, and the WORKSHOP WS25052A is the cheap way to make a big vac car-ready.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the vac sees sawdust or drywall dust, a cyclone separator is the most valuable thing you can add: the Dust Deputy DIY for small hoses, the Dustopper PRO for an easy 2-1/2 inch bucket-lid setup. For power tools, the POWERTEC 70376 hose kit connects sanders and saws without stacking adapters. Skip the 30-piece universal kits; one kit at your exact diameter beats a box of cones.
